10.text:
For a just identified model with one endogenous regressor

\begin{equation*}
\beta_{2SLS}-\beta=\frac{Y^{\prime}P_{Z}u}{Y^{\prime}P_{Z}Y}
\end{equation*}
and for normal errors the Nagar first order approximation is

\begin{equation*}
E\big[\beta_{2SLS}-\beta\big]\approx \frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\frac{k}{\mu^{2}},\qquad 
\mu^{2}=\frac{\pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\pi}{\sigma_{v}^{2}},
\end{equation*}
i.e.  up to the OLS bias

\begin{equation*}
{\rm RelBias}\approx\frac{k}{\mu^{2}} .
\end{equation*}
With $k=1$

\begin{equation*}
F=\frac{1}{k}\frac{\pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\pi}{\hat\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\;\approx\;\frac{\mu^{2}}{k}+1,
\qquad{\rm RelBias}\approx \frac1{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac1{F-1}\approx\frac1F ,
\end{equation*}
so that in the large sample limit ${\rm RelBias}<0.10$ is essentially
$\mu^{2}>10$, i.e. $F-1>10$.

Let $X\equiv kF$.  For homoscedastic normal errors, up to the
$ \hat\sigma_{v}^{2}\simeq\sigma_{v}^{2}$  approximation

\begin{equation*}
X\;\stackrel{a}{\sim}\;\chi^{2}_{k}(\mu^{2}),\qquad 
X=(Z+\sqrt{\mu^{2}})^{2} .
\end{equation*}
For $k=1$ $X\approx(\,N(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1)\,)^{2}$.  A one-sided
$1-\alpha$ lower confidence bound for the non-centrality $\mu^{2}$ is
therefore obtained from

\begin{equation*}
{\rm P}\!\left[X\le F_{obs}\mid\mu^{2}=\mu^{2}_{L}\right]
   =\Phi\!\left(\sqrt{F_{obs}}-\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{L}}\right)
    -\Phi\!\left(-\sqrt{F_{obs}}-\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{L}}\right)
   \approx 1-\alpha ,
\end{equation*}
i.e. in the normal approximation
$\sqrt{F_{obs}}\approx\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{L}}+z_{1-\alpha}$ and

\begin{equation*}
\mu^{2}_{L}\approx\bigl(\sqrt{F_{obs}}-z_{1-\alpha}\bigr)^{2}.
\end{equation*}
Putting the nominal one-sided $95\%$ normal quantile
$z_{0.95}=1.645$,
${\rm RelBias}<0.10\iff\mu^{2}_{L}>10$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{0.95}}\approx\sqrt{10}+z_{0.95},
\qquad 
F_{0.95}\approx\Bigl(\sqrt{10}+1.645\Bigr)^{2}\approx 23.1,
\end{equation*}
i.e. about $F_{obs}\simeq 22\!-\!24$ is required in order that the
$95\%$ lower fiducial limit for $\mu^{2}$ and hence for the Nagar
relative bias $k/\mu^{2}\approx 1/(F-1)$ lie below $0.10$.  In practice
this is quoted as the familiar $F\gtrsim 10$ point estimate,
$F\approx F_{0.95}\simeq 20\!-\!25$ for a $95\%$ confidence statement.
With the conventional two-sided $z_{0.975}=1.96$ this becomes
$F_{0.95}\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx 25$, essentially the
$F\approx 22\!-\!25$ rule of thumb that is usually quoted as
$F\approx 20\!-\!25$, e.g. $F\approx22$ for the one-sided $1.645$
normal approximation and $F\approx24\!-\!25$ for the usual
$1.96$ two-sided $95\%$ interval.  This is the $F\approx10$ rule
augmented by the usual $\sim\!1.6\!-\!2.0$ standard-error safety
factor.

3.text:
In the just identified homoskedastic model

$ y=X\beta +u\qquad X=Z\pi +v ,$
$\displaystyle \hat\beta_{IV}-\beta =\frac{Z'u}{Z'X} $

put $N_{n}=n^{-1/2}Z'u$ and $D_{n}= n^{1/2}\pi Q_{ZZ}+n^{-1/2}Z'v$.
With $E[N_{n}]=0$ and $E[N_{n}D_{n}]=\sigma_{uv}Q_{ZZ}$

\begin{equation*}
E\Big[\frac{N_{n}}{D_{n}}\Big]=\frac{E[N_{n}\delta]}{D_{0}^{2}}+o(n^{-1})
     =-\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{nQ_{ZZ}\pi^{2}}+o(n^{-1})
\end{equation*}

i.e. up to $O(n^{-1})$

\begin{equation*}
E[\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta]\simeq -\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{nQ_{ZZ}\pi^{2}} .
\end{equation*}

For OLS $E[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]=\sigma_{uv}/
\big(\pi^{2}Q_{ZZ}+\sigma_{v}^{2}\big)$.
Hence with 
$\mu^{2}=n\pi^{2}Q_{ZZ}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho_{N}\equiv
\frac{\big|E[\hat\beta_{IV}-\beta]\big|}
     {\big|E[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]\big|}
   =\frac{\pi^{2}Q_{ZZ}+\sigma_{v}^{2}}{n\pi^{2}Q_{ZZ}}
    =\frac1{\mu^{2}}+\frac1n\;\approx\;\frac1{\mu^{2}},
\end{equation*}

and in the just identified case $\mu^{2}\simeq F_{1}$ in large samples.
Up to first order

\begin{equation*}
\rho_{N}\;\approx\;F^{-1},
\end{equation*}

so that point wise $F>10$ is $10\%$ relative Nagar bias.

Let $F_{1}\approx \chi^{2}_{1}(\mu^{2})=(Z+\sqrt{\mu^{2}})^{2},$
$Z\sim N(0,1)$ .  For large $d_{2}$ $F_{1}$ can be written as
$\sqrt{F_{1}}\approx N\big(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1\big)$.
A $95\%$ one sided lower confidence bound for $\mu^{2}$ is obtained from

\begin{equation*}
P\!\left(\sqrt{F_{1}}\ge \sqrt{\mu^{2}}+z_{0.95}\;\big|\;\mu^{2}\right)=0.05,
\qquad z_{0.95}=1.645,
\end{equation*}

i.e. from the $95{\rm th}$ percentile of $F_{1}$ under the null
$\mu^{2}_{0}=10$,

\begin{equation*}
F^{*}\approx\big(\sqrt{\mu^{2}_{0}}+z_{0.95}\big)^{2}
      =\Big(\sqrt{10}+1.645\Big)^{2}\approx 2.3\times10^{1},
\end{equation*}

$F^{*}\approx23$ and

\begin{equation*}
\rho_{N}\lesssim \frac1{F^{*}} \approx0.10
\end{equation*}

with $95\%$ probability.  With the usual $z_{0.975}=1.96$ normal
approximation this is $F^{*}\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx26$,
i.e. roughly $20\!-\!25$; the familiar point Nagar rule
$\rho_{N}\approx F^{-1}<0.10\iff F>10$ is recovered for the median.
In the textbook point–estimate form the $10\%$ Nagar rule is
$F\gtrsim 10$ – about $12$ with the $F-2$ small–sample correction –
and the $95\%$ one–sided Nagar bound is the $\approx 23$, say
$20\!-\!26$, first–stage $F$ quoted above.  This is often quoted as
the $F\approx 16\!-\!20$ Stock–Yogo $10\%$ relative-bias weak-instrument
threshold.

