3.text:
Under homoscedasticity with $L=1$

\begin{equation*}
{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]\simeq\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\frac{L}{\mu^{2}}\qquad 
\mu^{2}=\frac{\pi^{\prime}Z^{\prime}Z\pi}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\end{equation*}
so that with ${\rm plim}(\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta)=\sigma_{uv}/\sigma_{v}^{2}$

\begin{equation*}
\rho_{N}(\mu^{2})\equiv
\frac{{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{2SLS}-\beta]}
     {{\rm E}[\hat\beta_{OLS}-\beta]}\simeq
\frac{L}{\mu^{2}}\simeq\frac1{\mu^{2}},
\end{equation*}
and in the large sample limit

\begin{equation*}
F=\frac{\hat\pi^{2}\sum z_{i}^{2}}{\hat\sigma_{v}^{2}}
\approx 1+\mu^{2},\qquad 
\sqrt F\stackrel{a}{\sim}N\!\left(\sqrt{\mu^{2}},1\right) .
\end{equation*}
Hence, up to the $1$ in $F\simeq1+\mu^{2}$,

\begin{equation*}
\rho_{N}\approx\frac1{\mu^{2}}\approx\frac1{F},
\qquad 
\rho_{N}^{U}\approx\frac1{\bigl(\sqrt{F}-z_{1-\alpha}\bigr)^{2}},
\end{equation*}
the $1-\alpha$ one-sided upper bound for the Nagar relative bias being
obtained from the $1-\alpha$ lower bound
$\sqrt{\mu^{2}}_{L}\simeq\sqrt F-z_{1-\alpha}$ for $\sqrt{\mu^{2}}$.
Putting $\rho_{N}^{U}=0.10$ i.e. $\mu^{2}_{L}=10$ gives

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{1-\alpha}}=\sqrt{10}+z_{1-\alpha}\;,
\end{equation*}
so that on the usual one-sided $95\%$ normal normalisation
$z_{0.95}=1.645$

\begin{equation*}
\sqrt{F_{0.95}}\approx\sqrt{10}+1.645\approx3.162+1.645=4.81,
\end{equation*}
i.e.

\begin{equation*}
F_{0.95}\approx\bigl(\sqrt{10}+1.645\bigr)^{2}\approx 23.1\approx23\!-\!24,
\end{equation*}
the $F-1\simeq\mu^{2}$ small-sample correction being
$F_{0.95}\simeq(\sqrt{10}+1.645)^{2}+1\approx24$.
With the conventional two-sided $z_{0.975}=1.96$ normal quantile this
is $F_{0.95}^{2sd}\approx(\sqrt{10}+1.96)^{2}\approx26.2\approx26$.
The median point Nagar rule $\rho_{N}\approx1/F=0.10$ is of course
$F_{med}\approx10$, $F_{med}-1\approx11$, the familiar Stock–Yogo
$10\%$ maximal-relative-bias $5\%$ size critical value being
$F^{*}\approx16.38\approx16$–$17$ on the median scale. ∎

